// book-demo — a guided tour of the `book` package, texish's LaTeX `book`-class format.
//
// The book package IS the document format, so we load only it (it pulls in the
// document package itself for emphasis, lists, quotations, floats and references).
// Loading `document` as well would double up the sectioning machinery.
\use{book}

// \title / \author / \date fill the doctitle / docauthor / docdate macros that the
// title page sets large and centred. They are inherited from the document package.
\title{The Book of Examples}
\author{A. N. Author}
\date{Typeset with texish}

// ===== Front matter =============================================================
// \frontmatter restarts the folio at lowercase-roman i and leaves chapters
// unnumbered. \maketitle then gives the title block a page of its own.
\frontmatter
\maketitle

// A table of contents. \contents prints the "Contents" heading and replays every
// numbered chapter, section and subsection collected on the first pass, each with a
// dotted leader to the page it lands on. Because texish resolves the page numbers by
// re-running the document until they settle, the numbers below are the real ones.
\contents

\chapter{Preface}

This book is set with the \mono{book} package, the \mono{book}-class companion to
the \mono{document} package's article format. Front matter such as this preface
carries lowercase-roman folios and unnumbered chapter titles, exactly as in LaTeX.

Look at the foot of the page: the folio is a roman numeral. The chapter title above
carries no number, because front-matter chapters are unnumbered. When the main matter
begins, the folios restart at arabic~1 and chapters take numbers, with sections
numbered \italic{within} their chapter.

The running head along the top of every page names the chapter it belongs to. It is
driven by a page mark that each chapter records, so turning a page never leaves the
head naming the wrong chapter.

\chapter{Acknowledgements}

A second front-matter chapter, also unnumbered, showing that the roman folio simply
keeps counting across chapters. Front matter is where a real book puts its preface,
foreword, acknowledgements and contents — everything before the numbered body.

// ===== Main matter ==============================================================
// \mainmatter restarts the folio at arabic 1 and turns chapter numbering on. Parts
// and chapters from here on are numbered.
\mainmatter

// A part is a page of its own: "Part I" over the part title, both centred. Parts group
// chapters without renumbering them — the chapter counter runs straight through.
\part{Foundations}

\chapter{Introduction}

The main matter numbers its chapters and has restarted the folio at one. This is
Chapter~1, and the page at the foot now reads an arabic numeral. Sections beneath a
chapter carry the chapter as a prefix, so the first section below is numbered 1.1.

Everything the document package provides is inherited unchanged: emphasis, lists,
quotations, floats and cross-references all work here without redeclaring anything. The
book package changes only the sectioning, the title page and the page numbering.

\section{Background}

A section's number is the chapter number and the section number joined with a dot. A
new chapter resets the section counter, so the first section of every chapter is always
\italic{n}.1. The running head at the top of the page shows the chapter title, recorded
as a page mark when the chapter opened.

\subsection{A finer point}

Subsections add a third level, numbered within the section: this one is 1.1.1. The
document package's lists, quotations and emphasis are inherited, so a book uses them
without any extra declarations.

\begin{quote}
A quotation block is indented on both sides and reverts to the body margins when it
ends, because the environment sets its margins inside its own group. This is the
document package's environment, used here unchanged inside a book chapter.
\end{quote}

\subsection{A second finer point}

This subsection numbers as 1.1.2 — the third level counts within its section. Were a new
section to begin, this subsection counter would restart at one alongside the section's.

\section{A second section}

This second section of the introduction numbers as 1.2. Because figures and tables are
also numbered within the chapter, the figure below is Figure~1.1 — the first figure of
Chapter~1 — and a figure in the next chapter would restart at 2.1.

\figure{
\centerline{\vrule width:120pt height:48pt}
\caption{A solid rule standing in for an illustration.}
\label{fig:rule}
}

The figure above is \autoref{fig:rule}, written with \mono{\\autoref}, which prints
the kind word and the per-chapter number the caption recorded. Cross-references resolve
on the same settling pass that fixes the contents page numbers.

\begin{itemize}
\item Chapters open a fresh page and set a running head.
\item Sections, subsections, figures and tables number within the chapter.
\item Front matter is roman and unnumbered; the main matter restarts at arabic one.
\end{itemize}

\chapter{A second chapter}

This is Chapter~2. Its sections restart at 2.1, and the running head at the top of the
page now names this chapter rather than the introduction. The folio at the foot keeps
counting in arabic numerals from where the previous chapter left off.

\section{Carrying on}

A book numbers each chapter's sections from one, so this is section 2.1 even though it
is the document's fourth numbered section overall. The body text wraps and justifies as
the document package sets it; the book package leaves the paragraph machinery alone.

\subsection{Onward}

Subsection 2.1.1, here only to add enough material that the chapter spans more than a
single page, letting the running head and folio be seen to follow the chapter across a
page break. A book's running head and folios are most convincing across several pages,
so this chapter deliberately runs long.

The Knuth–Plass line breaker fills each paragraph, the page builder breaks the column at
a sensible point, and the running head and footer wrap whatever lands on the page. None
of that is the book package's doing — it inherits the whole typesetting pipeline and only
dresses the page with chapter heads, a title page and per-matter folios.

\section{A closing section}

Section 2.2, to show the section counter advancing within the chapter while the
subsection counter it owns has reset. Numbering that restarts per chapter is the single
most book-like thing the package does, and it falls out of one \mono{\\counterwithin}
declaration plus a redefinition of how the section number prints.

// A second part, to show parts carry their own uppercase-roman counter and do not
// renumber the chapters under them — the next chapter is still numbered in sequence.
\part{Practice}

\chapter{Putting it together}

This is Chapter~3, the first chapter of Part~II. The part divider before it stepped the
part counter to II but left the chapter counter alone, so the chapters number straight
through both parts. Parts are organisational only; they group chapters without resetting
their numbers.

\section{A final numbered section}

Section 3.1 — the section counter has reset to one for the new chapter, as always. With
three chapters across two parts, the contents page at the front lists every part,
chapter, section and subsection with its resolved page number.

// A starred chapter prints no number and records no contents entry, exactly like
// LaTeX's \chapter*. It still opens a fresh page and sets the running head.
\chapter*{An unnumbered interlude}

This chapter was written \mono{\\chapter*}, so it carries no number and does not appear
in the table of contents, yet it still begins on a fresh page and updates the running
head. Starred sectioning is how a book sets apart material that should not be numbered.

// ===== Appendices ===============================================================
// \appendix restarts the chapter counter and letters it, and renames the word the
// chapter opening announces — so the chapters after it read "Appendix A" while every
// \chapter, \section and caption around them is written exactly as before. Section
// numbers, float captions and contents entries all follow the letter, because each is
// built on \thechapter rather than on the counter.
\appendix

\chapter{Reference tables}

This is Appendix~A. It was written with the same \mono{chapter} command as every
chapter before it; only the counter and the announcing word changed.

\section{A lettered section}

Section A.1 — the section number is built on the chapter's letter, so the appendix
carries its own numbering through every level beneath it.

// ===== Back matter ==============================================================
// \backmatter unnumbers chapters again but keeps the arabic folio running, so an
// afterword, bibliography or index reads as a titled chapter without a number.
\backmatter

\chapter{Afterword}

Back matter unnumbers chapters again while keeping the arabic folio running, so an
afterword, bibliography or index reads as a titled chapter without a number while the
page count continues unbroken from the main matter.

That completes the tour: a title page, a contents list, roman front matter, two parts of
numbered chapters with per-chapter sections, figures and cross-references, a starred
interlude, a lettered appendix, a reference list and unnumbered back matter — the whole
LaTeX \mono{book} class, in one short document.

// The reference list opens as a chapter here rather than as a section: in a book both
// the bibliography and the index are divisions in their own right, so the book package
// overrides the two heading macros the document package sets them with.
\begin{thebibliography}
\bibitem{knuth} Knuth, Donald E. \italic{The \TeX{}book}. Addison-Wesley, 1984.
\bibitem{lamport} Lamport, Leslie. \italic{\LaTeX: A Document Preparation System}.
Addison-Wesley, second edition, 1994.
\end{thebibliography}
